+1.2 Write $P_0=P(U)$ and $R=\mathbb Z[1/|n|]$. First take the supplied smooth disk $D\subset B$. Apply the finite smooth disk-tube construction [F5] to this actual disk and its boundary collar. They give $N=D^2\times D^2$, product-shaped at its boundary. The framing at $P_0$ is zero. Indeed the small parallel disk is disjoint from $D$; boundary linking equals their intersection over $R$ in the $R$-homology ball. To check the latter identity here, two choices of bounding two-chain differ by a two-cycle, which bounds since $H_2(B;R)=0$; the chain intersection boundary formula makes their intersections with a proper relative cycle equal. Compare with the pushed-in boundary Seifert chain to get boundary linking. Thus the integral boundary linking is zero in $R$, hence zero in $\mathbb Z$. Put $Z=\overline{B\setminus N}$. Its boundary is the actual preferred-longitude filling $M(P_0)$. In the excisive collared decomposition $B=Z\cup N$, the overlap is $D^2\times S^1$. Mayer–Vietoris, with its connecting map represented by the meridian disk, gives $H_0(Z;R)=R$, $H_1(Z;R)=R[\mu_0]$ and $H_j(Z;R)=0$ for $j\ge2$. This uses only the stated $R$-homology of $B$. For a pseudo-slice disk the same construction applies smoothly with $R=\mathbb Z$. For a TOP disk this calculation is valid if its boundary-compatible zero-framed product normal neighborhood is actually supplied; this paragraph does not construct that neighborhood from local flatness. [F4, F5, given, construct, algebra]
 
