+3.1 Here is the full gluing homology calculation. The common surgery $M(P_0)$ has $H_j=R$ in degrees0,1,2,3 and zero otherwise. The product-union spine defining $E$ has the Mayer–Vietoris sequence for the common solid torus; it gives $H_3(E;R)=R^2$ with the two incoming oriented fundamental classes as basis, $H_4(E;R)=0$, and no homology above4. This is the same finite sequence as the accepted supplier's3.1, now in degree3: the solid torus has no degree2 or3 homology. In the sequence for $V=E\cup_{M(P_0)}Z$, the degree3 map sends $1$ to the first basis vector of $R^2$; the degree2 map sends $1$ to the first basis vector of $R^2$; the degree1 map is the diagonal inclusion $R\to R\oplus R$ with unit coordinates, since $\mu_0$ generates both groups. All three are injective and the degree0 difference map is injective. Exactness in every degree therefore gives $H_j(V;R)=R$ for $j=0,1,2,3$ and zero otherwise. The $M(K)$ inclusion is an isomorphism in degree3 by the second fundamental-class coordinate, degree2 by the second basis coordinate, and degree1 by multiplication by $n$, a unit in $R$. For the outgoing end, degree2 is multiplication by $n$ because $(1,n)$ becomes $n$ in the quotient by the first basis vector; degree1 is the unit1. In degree3 the oriented boundary of a relative fundamental chain of $E$ is the sum of its incoming components minus the outgoing component. Consequently its outgoing degree3 class is the sum of the incoming classes; after gluing $Z$, it becomes the second generator. Degree0 is an isomorphism for both connected ends. Thus BOTH boundary inclusions are homology isomorphisms in every degree. The exact marked degree1 relation is $[\mu_K]=n[\mu_{P(K)}]$, with the signs determined by the supplied peripheral orientations. It is equality up to the unit $n$, not literal equality for general $n$. This is the precise meaning of the cited CFHH 'rel meridians' convention. [F3, F4, step 1.2, step 2.1, algebra]
 
