+4.1 Suppose now that the supplied disk lies in a simply connected ball (standard TOP or pseudo-smooth) and that $P$ is strong winding $\pm1$. Van Kampen for $B=Z\cup N$ identifies $\pi_1B$ with $\pi_1Z/\langle\!\langle\mu_0\rangle\!\rangle$, so $\mu_0$ normally generates $\pi_1Z$. Strong winding says that $\eta$ normally generates the closure knot-complement group; its image therefore normally contains $\mu_0$ in $\pi_1Z$, since the knot exterior maps into $Z$. Hence $\eta$ normally generates $\pi_1Z$ too. The actual product-union construction of $E$ and van Kampen give $$\pi_1V=(\pi_1Z*\pi_1M(K))/\langle\!\langle\eta\mu_K^{-1}\rangle\!\rangle.$$ No injectivity of the gluing maps is assumed. Killing $\mu_K$ kills $\pi_1M(K)$ and then $\eta$, hence $\pi_1Z$; so $\mu_K$ normally generates $\pi_1V$. Killing $\mu_0$ instead kills $\pi_1Z$, hence $\eta=\mu_K$ and the other factor, so $\mu_0$ normally generates as well. The same $\mu_0$ is the outgoing satellite meridian in the actual infection boundary. Thus the image of either boundary group normally generates $\pi_1V$. The integer homology calculation is step3.1 with $n=\pm1$. The smooth pseudo-slice branch and the conditional supplied-neighborhood TOP branch follow with their actual categories; no smooth identification of a homotopy ball is used. [F2, F3, F4, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]
 
